# Line-ending policy (SPEC-22 CI-02).
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# The content bundle manifest stores a SHA-256 per file, computed over the file's
# BYTES on the machine that generated it (scripts/generate-bundle-manifest.py
# opens "rb"). ContentBundle.Verify() recomputes that hash from the text it reads
# back, so the check only agrees when the checkout has the same bytes as the
# generator saw.
#
# With no .gitattributes, Windows checks out CRLF while the manifest was generated
# from LF, so every hashed file mismatches and Verify() returned 1331 problems for
# a 1332-file bundle. The test CleanDirectory_LoadsKnownExamCountQuestionErrataAnd...
# passed on Linux and failed only on build-winforms, for a reason that had nothing
# to do with the content being wrong.
#
# The manifest is generated and verified on all three platforms, so the checkout
# must be byte-identical on all three. That means LF everywhere, for text git can
# detect.
#
# Do not switch content/ to CRLF: the hashes are already committed and a CRLF
# checkout is a different file to the verifier.

# Default: normalise to LF in the repository, check out LF on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary types git must never convert.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.xz binary
*.nupkg binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.ogg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary

# Content that is hashed: LF is mandatory, not a preference.
content/** text eol=lf
errata.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.cs text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
